In its Form 10-K for fiscal 2023, PepsiCo identifies water scarcity and water quality as a material risk in the “Risk Factors” section.  Specifically, the company warns that:  

•  PepsiCo and its business partners rely on water both to manufacture finished products and to grow or process key agricultural inputs.  
•  “Lack of available water of acceptable quality, actions by … governmental and non-governmental organizations, investors, customers and consumers on water scarcity and increasing pressure to conserve and replenish water in areas of scarcity and stress … can lead to” (among other things):  
   –  Supply chain disruption;  
   –  Adverse effects on PepsiCo’s operations or its partners’ operations;  
   –  Higher compliance costs and capital expenditures (e.g. investments in water-efficiency technologies);  
   –  Higher production costs (including less favorable water pricing);  
   –  Interruption, cessation or relocation of facilities;  
   –  Failure to meet PepsiCo’s water-use goals;  
   –  Damage to reputation arising from perceived inaction or non-compliance with water regulations.  
•  These water-related risks “can adversely affect our business.”  

